<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Samugarh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1658">1658</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-war-of-succession-1657-1661">Mughal war of succession (1657–1661)</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Samugarh was fought on 29 May 1658 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dara-shikoh">Dara Shikoh</a>, heir of the ailing Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a>, and his brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> and Murad Bakhsh. Aurangzeb's victory, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rao-chhatrasal-of-bundi">Rao Chhatrasal of Bundi</a> and many other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> allies of Dara were killed, decided the war of succession. Aurangzeb then occupied Agra, confined Shah Jahan and made himself emperor.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-samugarh.jpg" alt="Battle of Samugarh" width="320" height="235" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Payag · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Battle_of_Samugarh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a> fell seriously ill in September 1657, and his sons fought for the throne in the Mughal war of succession of 1657 to 1659. Samugarh was the second battle between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dara-shikoh">Dara Shikoh</a>, the eldest son and heir apparent, and his younger brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> and Murad Baksh, the third and fourth sons. Each side had around 50,000 to 60,000 men, but Aurangzeb was the better general and his army more experienced. Dara relied on his Hada Rajputs and the Saiyids of Barha; most of his army was hastily recruited, and he did not seek help from the nobility of his father's empire.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Dara opened with an hour of cannonade, then sent his cavalry under Rustam Khan against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>'s artillery under Saif Shikan Khan; Rustam died fighting and his men fled. Dara's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajputs</a>, in his vanguard and right wing, broke into Aurangzeb's van, wounded Murad and routed Aurangzeb's left wing before Aurangzeb's greater numbers checked them.</p><p>Seeing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajputs</a> fall, Dara dismounted from his elephant and left the field, although, according to Saqi Mustad Khan, he still controlled large reserves while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>'s army had grown tired. The artillery of both armies included iron-cased rockets, which the French traveller François Bernier, who was present, described.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Dara gathered his household and left Shahjahanabad, which fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> text records that Guru Har Rai deployed his army, the Akal Sena, at Goindwal to delay Aurangzeb's pursuit of Dara; Mughal historians do not record this, and contemporary Mughal scholars called reports of Sikh assistance in the war of succession a rumour. Aurangzeb went on to imprison Murad and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a>, and Dara escaped but was captured and executed in 1659.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Samugarh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bundi's Raos often fought Mewar and later served the Mughals: Rao Surjan surrendered Ranthambore to Akbar in 1569, and Rao Chhatrasal was killed at <b>Samugarh</b> in 1658 fighting for Dara Shikoh.</blockquote><a href="/state/bundi-state">Bundi State</a></li></ul></section>
